{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And if from the flock.</b> They said in Torat Kohanim: this adds to the first matter — thus far. The intention in this is to apply everything stated regarding the species of cattle that was not mentioned regarding the flock, such as semikhah and its laws and the rest of the derivations. Also to derive, if a matter is repeated that was already mentioned regarding the cattle — and therefore you will find that they derived all the particulars that were repeated. Also to give what is stated in this passage concerning what precedes it, such as slaughter on the north side, etc., and the rest of the derivations learned from an olah of the flock." "And in Torat Kohanim it concludes there: And why did He interrupt? In order to give respite to Moses. The Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, raised the difficulty that from here it appears that the question of why He interrupted was raised only on account of the fact that it is written ״וְאִם״ with a vav that adds, whereas there in Torat Kohanim (chapter 1) in the baraita that discusses that a reading preceded the speaking, it taught: I might think even for interruptions, etc., and what were the interruptions for, etc. — thus far — it implies that even without a vav the interruptions served a purpose, etc. — thus far — and he answered, etc., and his words are not satisfactory." "And the correct view in my eyes is this: the force and reasoning of the responder there is from here — for were it not for the extra vav stated here, I would say that the reason for the interruptions was not to give respite to Moses to reflect, but rather so that Moses would understand not to derive a law from this one to that one. And from the fact that we find that HaShem wrote here and said ״וְאִם מִן הַצֹּאן״ with the addition of a vav — you have learned that their law is all equal, and to apply what is stated of this one to this one. If so, why were there interruptions? It is necessary to say that it was to give respite to Moses. And from here, to every place where there are interruptions — behold, the reason is before you, and it is the statement of the Tanna in general terms: and what were the interruptions for?"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:52:33.540329370Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}